{
  "abstract": "RLVR on Qwen3-0.6B-Base plateaus. Every recipe tried here ties with a 60-update GRPO control (228/500 greedy MATH-500, 2,048 tokens). A common reading: the policy lacks information on items it never solves. We pre-registered five routes for putting information or self-checking into those items, and ran each on the 148 validation items where the control solves 0 of 8 samples. The routes were self-correction cues, distilled after-answer checks, reference sub-goal credit in GRPO, human references as SFT targets, and retrieved worked examples in context. Each arm is compared on a fresh pool against a matched arm. All five fail their frozen rules. No route reaches the +.03 pass@8 bar. Pooled over the four information contrasts, pass@8 changes by +.008 [-.030, .046] and mean p by +.0008. The one greedy result that passed (+12 items) did not replicate with a second SFT seed (-3). Post hoc, the bin is low-p, not zero-p: 12 fresh pools reach 76 of the 148 items, with pooled p = .034. Their pass@8 spread matches a within-item permutation null (P = .51). The ten pools that keep the control's format are not distinguishable on which items they reach. Each route changed something else. References collapse the output format. Sub-goal credit acts as a termination reward. Distilled checking installs the form of a check but not its function. Retrieval helps only on near-duplicate problems. Payoff arithmetic shows why success on this bin could not have produced a greedy advance. A +.03 pass@8 gain is worth about 0.6 greedy items, and raising all 148 items to one correct sample in eight is worth 18.6. The answer-level headroom is elsewhere: 37.8 of the control's 38.8-item majority-vote gap lies on items it already samples correctly 1-7 times. The remaining route is to raise held-out p in that band.",
